{#
  Interfaces rendered from NetBox. `interfaces` is the nb_inventory host var
  added by `interfaces: true`; the committed fixture mirrors its shape, so this
  renders identically from either source.

  Sorted by name: the rendered configs are committed and diffed, and unstable
  ordering would make every run look like a change.

  Skipped: interfaces with no IP address, and the management interface. Mgmt is
  the one carrying ansible_host, which nb_inventory sets from primary_ip4, so no
  separate marker is needed. Rendering it would put the address Ansible connects
  over into the file deploy.yml pushes.

  Admin state is deliberately asymmetric, because IOS is. `show running-config`
  prints `shutdown` for an interface that is down and prints nothing for one that
  is up, so a `no shutdown` line here could never match the running config and
  ios_config would re-send it on every deploy. Only the printable half is
  rendered: `shutdown` when NetBox marks the interface disabled, nothing when it
  does not. Bringing an interface up is handled by cisco.ios.ios_interfaces in
  deploy.yml, which reads the same NetBox `enabled` field and can compare against
  the device's current state instead of against config text.

  Drift comparison is unaffected in either direction and needs no drop pattern:
  an interface NetBox marks enabled but that is administratively down shows up as
  an unexpected `shutdown` on the device, and one NetBox marks disabled but that
  is up shows up as a missing `shutdown`.
#}
hostname {{ inventory_hostname }}
!
{% for iface in interfaces | sort(attribute='name') %}
{% if iface.ip_addresses %}
{% set address = (iface.ip_addresses | first).address %}
{% if address | ansible.utils.ipaddr('address') != ansible_host %}
interface {{ iface.name }}
 description {{ iface.description }}
 ip address {{ address | ansible.utils.ipaddr('address') }} {{ address | ansible.utils.ipaddr('netmask') }}
{% if not iface.enabled %}
 shutdown
{% endif %}
!
{% endif %}
{% endif %}
{% endfor %}
